course: dictionary
lesson: 1719
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Context tells you which meaning is right, so read the words around it first. «For fun, crowds cheered chariot teams at the Circus Maximus, like football fans, and watched gladiators in the Colosseum.» Chariot teams, gladiators and cheering fans tell you this is an ancient place for sport, not a show with clowns.
explain: «The kitchen was a circus this morning.» There is no tent or clown here. Ask: what word would fit? A noisy, messy situation fits. Swap that meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense: the kitchen was a noisy mess this morning. It does, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Some words are used in informal speech to make a point. «Some jump from one small step to a disaster: blue hair, then tattoos, then the circus.» Here the circus means something out of control, not a show. When a word seems odd, look at the feeling in the sentence, then check the dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: A circus can be a travelling show, often in a big tent, with acrobats, clowns, jugglers and sometimes trained animals.
fact: In the UK, a circus can also be a round open place where several streets meet, such as Piccadilly Circus in London.
fact: «Circus Maximus» was a huge place in ancient Rome where crowds cheered for chariot teams called the Reds, Whites, Greens and Blues.
fact: Informal use: calling something a circus means it is noisy, messy or silly, as in «a media circus».
fact: The plural of circus is circuses.
exercise: «For fun, crowds cheered chariot teams at the Circus Maximus.» Where were the crowds? | In a tent full of acrobats and jugglers | *At an ancient Roman sports place, cheering for chariots | At a busy road junction in London | In a travelling show with clowns
exercise: «The kitchen was a circus this morning.» What does circus mean here? | A travelling show in a big tent | A quiet place with lots of plants | A round open place where streets meet | *A noisy, messy situation
exercise: What is the plural of circus? | *circuses | circuss | circusses | circi
test: «A few hundred brown bears live in the Ukrainian Carpathians, and near Lake Synevyr a centre cares for bears rescued from cages, circuses and restaurants.» Which place in this sentence is a place bears were rescued from? | *circuses | Carpathians | centre | Synevyr
test: «Piccadilly Circus is always busy with buses and people.» Which meaning of circus fits here? | *A round open place where several streets meet | A chariot race | A noisy, messy situation | A travelling show with clowns
exam: «The Circus Maximus held perhaps 150,000 fans, who cheered for chariot teams called the Reds, Whites, Greens and Blues.» About how many fans could the Circus Maximus hold? | Only a few hundred | Two hundred and fifty million | *About 150,000 | Exactly 15
